<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yuki Hideyasu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1574">1574</a>–<a href="/years/1607">1607</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Matsudaira Hideyasu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yuki Hideyasu was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, adopted first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> and then by the Yuki family. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> his father gave him Echizen province in 1601, where he founded the domain later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> and rebuilt Kitanosho castle. His descendants ruled as the Echizen Matsudaira house.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuki-hideyasu.jpg" alt="Yuki Hideyasu" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuki_Hideyasu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hideyasu was born Tokugawa Ogimaru on 1 March 1574, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> by Lady <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, a handmaiden of his wife, Lady Tsukiyama. Fearing his wife's anger, Ieyasu had Oman sheltered in the house of his retainer Honda Shigetsugu near Hamamatsu Castle, where she is said to have borne twins. Ieyasu disliked the boy and first met him at the age of three, a meeting arranged by his elder half-brother Matsudaira Nobuyasu.</p><p>After Nobuyasu's forced seppuku Ogimaru stood next in line by birth, but in the peace that followed the Battle of Komaki-Nagakute he was given in 1584 to the childless Hashiba Hideyoshi, in name as an adopted son and in reality as a hostage. He came of age as Hashiba Hideyasu, a name joining those of both fathers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first campaign was the Kyūshū Campaign of 1587, in which he led the assault on Buzen-Iwaishi Castle and was honoured for the pacification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyuga-province">Hyūga Province</a>. He also served at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> in 1590 and in the Japanese invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, and earned respect as an able field commander despite his youth. When a son was born to Hideyoshi in 1589, Hideyoshi began placing his adopted heirs in other great houses, and in 1590 Hideyasu was adopted by Yūki Harutomo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimosa-province">Shimōsa Province</a>, married Harutomo's niece and succeeded to the Yūki headship and its holding of 101,000 koku.</p><p>At the time of the Battle of Sekigahara Ieyasu ordered him to stay in Shimōsa, possibly because of his sympathy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> or because his standing as a commander might overshadow his younger half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Tokugawa Hidetada</a>. His new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Echizen was rated at 670,000 koku. In 1604 he was allowed to take the surname Matsudaira, and in 1605 he rose to Senior Third Rank.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hideyasu died on 2 June 1607 at the age of 34, possibly of syphilis. Professor Sunao Kawaguchi suspected that he was passed over as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>'s successor because he had spent most of his life close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a>. His will urged his heir to support Toyotomi Hideyori even against the Tokugawa, but his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-tadanao">Matsudaira Tadanao</a> ignored it, and the Echizen-Matsudaira clan survived to the Meiji restoration of 1868. Other sons inherited the domains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsue">Matsue</a>, Himeji and Ōno.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yūki Hideyasu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1601 Tokugawa Ieyasu gave Echizen, with 680,000 koku, to his second son <b>Yuki Hideyasu</b>, who rebuilt the former castle of Shibata Katsuie at Kitanosho; the town was later renamed Fukui.</blockquote><a href="/state/fukui-domain">Fukui Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hamamatsu Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Fukui, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.71,137.73&amp;year=1595" aria-label="Hamamatsu Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Yuki Hideyasu"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4144 4551h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4144 4551h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.71,137.73&amp;year=1595">What was here in 1595? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
